/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490180 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490180 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490180 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186489853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490180 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c6b96e89-e07b-5a12-a9c0-374702448788" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490189 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490189 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490189 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490189 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186489814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490189 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "dc56acdc-bb5e-585d-b688-0b28d6cc7c09" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490210 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490210 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490210 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490210 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490210 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490210 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490210 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490210 #edb/ref 1089 #edb/ref 17592186490211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490210 #edb/ref 1090 #uuid "d35884e6-1669-5364-a41a-a52efb1220e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490211 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186490335 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490211 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186490337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490211 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186490345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490211 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186490349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490211 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186490357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490211 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186490384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490211 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186490387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490211 #edb/ref 1085 "A store in a small town sells candy." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490211 #edb/ref 1086 #uuid "3ef56e99-ee02-5a7a-b434-af1fab1ad5e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490211 #edb/ref 1087 #edb/ref 17592186490345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490223 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490124 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490223 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134280" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490223 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490223 #edb/ref 1071 "John bought $2$ pounds of chocolate almonds while Rose bought $\\frac{7}{5}$ pound of the same product. How many chocolate almonds did they buy altogether? Express your final answer as a mixed number.\n\nThey bought$\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$pounds of chocolate almonds." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490223 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "24d13ec7-deb1-5d3a-8434-3206289f496e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490223 #edb/ref 1074 "To determine the amount of chocolate almonds they bought in total, we have to calculate the following sum:\n\n$$\n2+\\frac{7}{5}\n$$\n\nTo add $2$ and $\\frac{7}{5},$ we need to express the whole number $2$ as an equivalent fraction with a denominator of $5.$\n\nFirst, we write $2$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{1}\n$$\n\nThen we put this fraction over a denominator of $5$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{2\\times 5}{1\\times 5}=\\frac{10}{5}\n$$\n\nSo now, we have to find:\n\n$$\n\\frac{10}{5}+\\frac{7}{5}\n$$\n\nTo add two fractions with like denominators, we add the numerators and keep the denominators the same:\n\n$$\n\\frac{10}{5}+\\frac{7}{5}=\\frac{10+7}{5}=\\frac{17}{5}\n$$\n\nExpressing our answer as a mixed number, we have\n\n$$\n17÷5=3\\,\\text{R}\\,2=3\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, John and Rose bought $3\\frac{2}{5}$ pounds of chocolate almonds." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490241 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490241 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134281" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490241 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490241 #edb/ref 1071 "Of the $2$ pounds of chocolate almonds that John bought, he gave his cousin Angela $\\frac{5}{6}$ pounds. As a mixed number, John had{{field-1}}\n$\\,$pounds of chocolate almonds remaining." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490241 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "40b45065-d83b-509a-aac4-0020a7f5f19d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490241 #edb/ref 1074 "To find out how many chocolate almonds John had left, we must calculate the difference $2-\\frac{5}{6}.$\n\nTo subtract $\\frac{5}{6}$ from $2,$ we need to express the whole number $2$ as an equivalent fraction with a denominator of $6.$\n\nFirst, we write $2$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{1}\n$$\n\nThen, we put this fraction over a denominator of $6$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $6:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{2\\times 6}{1\\times 6}=\\frac{12}{6}\n$$\n\nSo now, we have to find:\n\n$$\n\\frac{12}{6}-\\frac{5}{6}\n$$\n\nTo subtract two fractions with like denominators, we subtract the numerators and keep the denominators the same. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{12}{6}-\\frac{5}{6}=\\frac{12-5}{6}=\\frac{7}{6}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n7÷6=1\\,\\text{R}\\,1=1\\,\\frac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, John has $1\\,\\frac{1}{6}$ pounds of chocolate almonds left." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490268 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490176 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490268 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134277" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490268 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490268 #edb/ref 1071 "Peter, Steve, Chelsea, and Laura each bought $3$ ounces of gummies and $4$ ounces of chocolates. In total, they bought$\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ounces of candy." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490268 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7a9fb5ec-479f-5a41-8212-79254e42e93a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490268 #edb/ref 1074 "We must write and evaluate an expression representing the total amount of candy.\n\nSince each of them bought $3$ ounces of gummies and $4$ ounces of chocolates, the amount of candy each person bought, in ounces, is:\n\n$$\n{{3}_{⏟}}_{\\text{gummies}}+{{4}_{⏟}}_{\\text{chocolates}}\n$$\n\nSince *each* person purchased this amount of candy, and there are $4$ people, we must multiply the amount of candy by $4.$ Therefore, the total amount of candy is:\n\n$$\n{{(3+4)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{each person}}\\times {{4}_{⏟}}_{\\text{people}}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the expression, we follow the order of operations with parentheses.\n\nFirst, we perform the operations inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3+4)\\times 4 & =(3+4)\\times 4 \\\\  & =7\\times 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply:\n\n$$\n7\\times 4=28\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490269 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490269 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134283" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490269 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490269 #edb/ref 1071 "Rose decides to eat $\\frac{2}{5}$ of her chocolate almonds. How many pounds of almonds does she decide to eat?\n\n*Remember that Rose has $\\frac{7}{5}$ of a pound of chocolate almonds.*\n\nRose decides to eat\n{{field-1}}\npounds of chocolate almonds." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490269 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7b9cad24-adba-5e25-92cf-4f2a7c2a1894" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490269 #edb/ref 1074 "We know that Rose has $\\frac{7}{5}$ of a pound of chocolate almonds.\n\nTo calculate the fraction of a whole pound that Rose decides to eat, we need to multiply $\\frac{2}{5}$ by $\\frac{7}{5}.$\n\nTo multiply two fractions, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{5}\\times \\frac{7}{5}=\\frac{2\\times 7}{5\\times 5}=\\frac{14}{25}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, Rose decides to eat $\\frac{14}{25}$ pounds of chocolate almonds." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490271 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490271 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134278" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490271 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490271 #edb/ref 1071 "There are $14$ boxes of lollipops in stock, each box containing $22$ lollipops. Therefore, there are $\\,${{field-1}}\n$\\,$lollipops in the store." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490271 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "83ea9ab0-b2f6-54ee-a35a-5ac8d6525a5a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490271 #edb/ref 1074 "To calculate the total number of lollipops, we need to multiply $14$ by $22.$\n\nSo, we write it this way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  &  &  &  & \\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the ones:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned} \\\\ \\boxed{0}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} & \\,\\, & \\begin{aligned}14\\times 2=28\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply the tens:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{0}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} & \\,\\, & \\begin{aligned}14\\times 20=280\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add the results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{0}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} & \\,\\, & \\begin{aligned}28+280=308\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $14\\times 22=308.$\n\nTherefore, there are $308$ lollipops in total." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490298 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490298 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490298 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134282" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490298 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490298 #edb/ref 1071 "After Angela takes her share of John's chocolate almonds, the weight of John's remaining chocolate almonds is$\\,${{field-1}}\n$\\,$than the weight of Rose's chocolate almonds because $1\\frac{1}{6}$\n{{field-2}} $\\frac{7}{5}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490298 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bc49bd25-7e09-53de-af63-f601d564b8e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490298 #edb/ref 1074 "John has $1\\frac{1}{6}$ pounds of chocolate almonds remaining, and Rose has $\\frac{7}{5}$ pounds of this product.\n\nWe cannot compare the fractions directly as they don't have a common denominator.\n\nWe can make a common denominator of $6\\times 5=30.$\n\nTo put $1\\frac{1}{6}$ over a denominator of $30,$ we first convert to an improper fraction and then multiply the numerator and denominator by $5:$\n\n$$\n1\\frac{1}{6}=\\frac{7}{6}=\\frac{7\\times 5}{6\\times 5}=\\frac{35}{30}\n$$\n\nTo put $\\frac{7}{5}$ over a denominator of $30,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator by $6:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{5}=\\frac{7\\times 6}{5\\times 6}=\\frac{42}{30}\n$$\n\nWe can now compare the fractions $\\frac{35}{30}$ and $\\frac{42}{30}$ by looking at the numerators only.\n\nSince $35<42,$ the correct statement must be:\n\n$$\n\\frac{35}{30}<\\frac{42}{30}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is\n\n> \"After Angela takes her part of John's chocolate almonds, the weight of John's remaining chocolate almonds is $<$ than the weight of Rose's chocolate almonds because $1\\frac{1}{6}<\\frac{7}{5}.$\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490313 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490189 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490313 #edb/ref 1069 "q-134279" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490313 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490313 #edb/ref 1071 "There are $308$ lollipops at the store. The store manager wants to make packages containing $4$ lollipops each. In total, he can make$\\,${{field-1}}\n$\\,$packages." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490313 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dfff5d32-78f7-5797-b312-14bdc373c270" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490313 #edb/ref 1074 "We know that there are $308$ lollipops in total. Since each package must contain $4$ lollipops,the store manager can make $308÷4$ packages.\n\nWe start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"150\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"2\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{3\\,\\phantom{|}0\\,\\phantom{|}8}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n<td align=\"left\" width=\"250\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr height=\"20\">\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nNotice that $3$ (the number of hundreds in $308$) is less than the divisor $4.$ Therefore, we should consider both the hundreds and tens:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"150\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$7$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{3\\,\\phantom{|}0\\,\\phantom{|}8}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$-\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$8$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$↓$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n<td align=\"left\" width=\"250\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr height=\"20\">\n<td>Divide: $30÷4=7\\,\\text{R}\\,2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"20\">\n<td>Write over tens: $7$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"25\">\n<td>Multiply: $4\\times 7=28$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"20\">\n<td>Subtract: $30-28=2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"25\">\n<td>Bring down: $8$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nNext, we consider the ones:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"150\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$7$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$7$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{3\\,\\phantom{|}0\\,\\phantom{|}8}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$-\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$8$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$-\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\boxed{\\text{0}}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></t